-### Temporal Encoding of Indexed Volumetric Data
-This method allows the temporal encoding of indexed volumetric data to enable quick hardware decoding, utilizing OpenGL color palette manipulation to represent grid points at several time steps using a single scalar value [S2, unofficial].
-
-*   **Mechanism:** The method uses an extensive use of OpenGL to change color palettes without reloading textures. The palette consists of limited sets of colors that sample a single value. The palette is varied to decode the approximate scalar value of a voxel for each frame properly. The scalar value is then mapped to the desired opacity and color scalar index. A sequence of temporally changing scalar values is encoded inside a texel, where each possible index value represents a sample in the space of possible time-varying scalar values. Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) is used to map sequences of scalars into single scalar indices [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Distinction:** It allows the representation of grid points at several times using a single scalar value, whereas standard methods might require storing full time-series data. It achieves a compression ratio of 5:1 for volume data of $128^3$ [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to choose:** When hardware decoding speed is critical and the data can be effectively indexed and quantized into a limited color palette.
-*   **Link:** No dedicated article.
+A method for temporal encoding of indexed volumetric data allowing quick hardware decoding, using OpenGL color palette manipulation to represent grid points at several times using a single scalar value [S2, unofficial]. The palette consists of limited sets of colors that sample a single value. The palette is varied to decode the approximate scalar value of a voxel for each frame properly, after which the scalar value is mapped to the desired opacity and color scalar index [S2, unofficial]. The method encodes a sequence of temporally changing scalar values inside a texel, where each possible index value represents a sample in the space of possible time-varying scalar values. Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) is used to map sequences of scalars into single scalar indices [S2, unofficial]. This allows representation of grid points at several times using a single scalar value, whereas standard methods might require storing full time-series data [S2, unofficial]. Tested on an NVIDIA GeForce2GTS graphics card with 64 megabytes of texture memory, achieving a compression ratio of 5:1 for volume data of $128^3$ [S2, unofficial]. Some blockiness and contouring were reported [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The need for time-varying volume compression arises from the bandwidth limitations at various stages of the rendering pipeline, which often make it infeasible to interactively load, decode, and render each time step of a volumetric animation on the GPU in real time [S1]. Consequently, compression solutions are designed to pre-load entire animations into graphics memory [S1].
-
-The three main strategies (4D extension, key-frame/differential, and per-step) were identified in the context of real-time rendering of time-varying volumetric datasets [S1]. The temporal encoding of indexed volumetric data using OpenGL palette manipulation was developed by Eric B. Lum et al., who demonstrated interactive rendering rates for large time-varying datasets on an NVIDIA GeForce2GTS graphics card with 64 MB of texture memory [S2, unofficial].
+The need for compression in time-varying volume rendering arises from bandwidth limitations at various stages of the rendering pipeline, making it often infeasible to interactively load, decode, and render each time step on the GPU in real time [S1]. Consequently, compression solutions are designed to pre-load entire animations into graphics memory [S1]. Three main compression strategies have been identified for the time-varying domain: 4D extension, key-frame/differential encoding, and per-step encoding [S1]. The 4D extension approach has been explored in works such as [ILRS03, WWS03, WWS*05] [S1]. The key-frame/differential approach is similar to video encoding techniques [S1]. The temporal encoding of indexed volumetric data using OpenGL palette manipulation was developed by Eric B. Lum et al. [S2, unofficial].
 
